So the main thing I wanted to chat through today, and there's so much depth that it will probably take up the whole time just talking about this.

53
00:04:04,240 --> 00:04:09,040
But you're kind of internet famous for working on this.

54
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:16,720
I mean, yeah, I'm so plugged into the subscription app world and like I just eat, live and breathe subscription apps.

55
00:04:16,960 --> 00:04:26,880
And so when, when this ethical design pattern that you helped create hit Twitter and the subclub community.

56
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:30,880
that I run, it just was like huge buzz.

57
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:32,480
And then now Apple uses it.

58
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:41,920
And so it really was like a pretty big kind of moment in the subscription app space of like, wow, this is like a whole different way to approach paywalls.

59
00:04:42,160 --> 00:04:50,080
So that's what I wanted to dig into today, and I wanted to start with understanding the product discovery process.

60
00:04:50,720 --> 00:05:09,760
So I think this is where a lot of subscription apps maybe rely a little bit too heavily on patterns they've seen before versus actually talking to customers, doing user research and just brainstorming, what are things we think might work?

61
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:18,560
And so I listed a couple of things, user research and brainstorming, but I'm sure there's at Blink, at a company like Blinkist, there's a whole process around it.

62
00:05:18,720 --> 00:05:26,560
So I wanted to kind of talk through that product discovery process or feature discovery process at Blinkist.

63
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:36,000
Yeah, with this problem specifically, like I took it from like when I first started, basically, I saw that this was the biggest problem that they had at Blinkist.

64
00:05:36,640 --> 00:05:41,360
And a lot of people at the company knew this, but exactly, basically they said, well, it's an industry wide thing.

65
00:05:41,520 --> 00:05:42,960
This is happening to a lot of people of people.

66
00:05:43,120 --> 00:05:44,000
This has probably happened to you.

67
00:05:44,160 --> 00:05:46,000
It's happened to a lot of people I've spoken with.

68
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:47,920
So it was just like this is the normal.

69
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:51,680
And what was the problem specifically that you were looking at?

70
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:52,560
Yeah, yeah.

71
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:54,640
I guess we should talk about the actual problem first too.

72
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:54,880
Yeah.

73
00:05:55,920 --> 00:06:11,120
So here was that, um, we would offer this seven day trial right at the beginning and then people would sign up for this trial, give their credit card and then on day seven they would be charged unexpectedly sometimes so they forget to cancel, um, maybe haven't used the app or just deleted the app.

74
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:15,200
And then they're writing these complaints being like, hey, I was charged for this.

75
00:06:15,680 --> 00:06:20,320
And of course, like, like, my empathy, I was just like, I feel so bad that this is happening.

76
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:22,160
to so many people out there.

77
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:23,520
It's such a common thing.

78
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:26,720
So yeah, that was the problem that we were trying to tackle.

79
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:31,040
It's less people complaining that, yeah, they're charged without knowing.

80
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:32,000
Yeah.

81
00:06:32,240 --> 00:06:48,160
And imagine that was both a drag on on reviews that you would get reviews that share, you know, hey, this is, and I think that there is, there are some, you know, three day free trials are really confusing with Apple because it actually charges like two days in.

82
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:56,800
And so people sometimes do genuinely feel scammed when the free trial hits earlier than they expect or they forget.

83
00:06:57,680 --> 00:07:02,880
So it's a drag on reviews., but I imagine it was also a customer support burden.

84
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:06,560
So, you know, customer support probably had to handle a lot of these.

85
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:18,400
And then, oh, and I mean, just to complain a little bit, Apple doesn't make this easy either because you have to actually tell them, go to Apple and ask for a refund.

86
00:07:18,560 --> 00:07:23,760
You can't just have that great customer experience of giving them a refund directly.

87
00:07:24,320 --> 00:07:24,640
Exactly.

88
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:36,720
And then the third thing, you know, was there kind of user research around this as well, um, to understand what, how people actually felt about this free trial and getting canceled and not being able to cancel.

89
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:52,160
So we had a researcher that was collecting this data from like NPS surveys that we were doing and then also from the App Store reviews, also Google Play reviews from like different sources, also from customer service complaints and kind of made this report every month.

90
00:07:52,320 --> 00:07:54,480
And like every time it would be that this was the highest thing.

91
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:56,000
So it was kind of gathering those things.

92
00:07:56,400 --> 00:07:56,480
Wow.

93
00:07:57,200 --> 00:07:58,960
So it was very clear that this was happening.

94
00:07:59,360 --> 00:07:59,440
Yeah.

95
00:08:00,080 --> 00:08:11,840
But then, yeah, it wasn't until like way later when I really pushed this as more like, okay, this is can be better for the business if we have less complaints about this, let's research this a bit more.

96
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:18,560
Um, so then we worked with another researcher as well to do like a survey, talk to users and get more information on this as well.

97
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:19,680
Yeah.

98
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:29,360
And so then there were a few experiments you did to early on to try and ameliorate this, including even just removing the free trial.

99
00:08:29,520 --> 00:08:31,440
So how how did that experiment go?

100
00:08:32,400 --> 00:08:32,480
Yeah.

101
00:08:32,800 --> 00:08:46,000
So we had tried in the past, like, yeah, just completely or just having the paywall right away and not really having an app experience that you could use or just before like years ago, we didn't actually have this seven day trial.

102
00:08:46,160 --> 00:08:49,040
You could just try the app for three days or seven days or something like that.

103
00:08:49,680 --> 00:08:52,400
And once we implemented this, you know, seven day trial.

104
00:08:52,400 --> 00:08:54,160
And then in the 10-day trial conversion went way higher.

105
00:08:54,240 --> 00:08:57,680
And with all these experiments trying to get rid of it, it was just terrible.

106
00:08:57,840 --> 00:09:03,600
It was just, it was, when the conversion went so much down, it was just like impossible to keep the business going with this.

107
00:09:03,840 --> 00:09:04,240
So yeah.

108
00:09:05,600 --> 00:09:29,600
And then I imagine it is a, it is a really tough thing in this role of trying to balance the business objectives because in some ways, and this is always a little bit of tension with subscription app businesses is that there just is by default going to be a certain number of people who don't intend to be subscribed.

109
00:09:30,160 --> 00:09:38,800
And I I call them zombie subscribers, like they haven't used the app in six months, nine months, a year, but they're still subscribed because they forget or whatever.

110
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:51,040
And so there's a little bit of tension between the kind of business objective of like we're a for profit company and if someone's paying us, we don't want not to take their money.

111
00:09:51,760 --> 00:10:00,160
But then on the other side, there's the kind of, you know, ethical and even just kind of user experience side of we want people to have a good experience.

112
00:10:00,320 --> 00:10:03,680
So if they don't, if they're not intending to pay us, we don't want to take their money.

113
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:14,800
So how did you kind of think through and start trying to figure out how to balance the kind of business and ethics and customer empathy as a product designer.

114
00:10:15,680 --> 00:10:19,760
Yeah, with your point too about like the people that have been subscribed for six months as well.

115
00:10:19,920 --> 00:10:24,080
That was another thing that we started to tackle later too that people didn't know how to cancel.

116
00:10:24,480 --> 00:10:26,000
Like how do they cancel in the app?

117
00:10:26,640 --> 00:10:30,160
So they kept keep on subscribing, but that was like another project that we did later.

118
00:10:30,640 --> 00:10:31,840
Well, let's talk about that later.

119
00:10:34,720 --> 00:10:35,600
That was also interesting.

120
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:47,600
But yeah, so back to the question of like, yeah, I had this like designer brain that I've always known that's like, okay, always like stand up for the user, do what's right for the user and wasn't really thinkinging about the business.

121
00:10:47,840 --> 00:10:50,240
Like when I first started at Blink, it's like this business case.

122
00:10:50,560 --> 00:10:56,160
And I was like, for the user, we have to get rid of this subscription stuff, you know, like just get rid of it.

123
00:10:56,320 --> 00:10:56,880
It's too bad.

124
00:10:57,120 --> 00:11:01,440
And then, uh, everyone's like, no, like we need, as you said, like it's like capitalism.

125
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:07,600
We need to, we need to make money for the money for this business and so that they can pay our salary as well.

126
00:11:08,160 --> 00:11:10,480
Um, so I, it was like nine months later.

127
00:11:10,640 --> 00:11:11,600
I was like, still thinking about this.

128
00:11:11,760 --> 00:11:15,840
We're working on other problems that were not as, you know, big of problems, but still doing that.

129
00:11:16,160 --> 00:11:18,880
And then I took this approach of like, what is good for the business in this case?

130
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:24,480
And that's how I pitched it that we can decrease these customer service complaints so there's less burden on that team.

131
00:11:25,200 --> 00:11:30,800
And then also that there's less of these complaints and then we have more growth and like people actually downloading the app in the app store.

132
00:11:31,360 --> 00:11:50,080
So yeah, it was like finding that balance between saying and also just good for team morale and ethics of course as well that if we did something to try to tackle this problem then when I took that approach of this kind of like finding that middle ground then we started to work on this project and yeah had a workshop to get everyone on board and excited about this.

133
00:11:50,160 --> 00:11:50,560
So yeah.

134
00:11:51,440 --> 00:11:54,240
Early on, what was the pitch?

135
00:11:54,960 --> 00:12:15,360
So you had the problem was the pitch the specific idea that then became this ethical design pattern or what was the kind of first pitch and then how did you start to iterate toward what we eventually saw in the in the blog post you shared and ultimately on the app store?

136
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:17,600
So yeah, it was definitely like a team thing.

137
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:26,640
This wasn't just like an idea that I had after seeing all this, like we took all this research, I made sure to like, yeah, work with this researcher, put everything together and then had this workshop.

138
00:12:27,280 --> 00:12:33,440
In this workshop, we put all of our minds on our team was called the Cartoffel team and that was working on conversion and growth.

139
00:12:33,920 --> 00:12:40,960
And we all kind of came up with ideas in this workshop that I ran and the end decision that we all wanted to work on was the following.

140
00:12:40,960 --> 00:12:44,160
And one of the things we all wanted to work on was this kind of like timeline and trial reminders concept.

141
00:12:45,520 --> 00:13:01,200
But the pitch was more and then for this, like, yeah, it was like this backing up with this research and saying, and then coming out of this, of this workshop as well, saying, okay, we're going to decrease customer service complaints by ten percent and we think that will be beneficial for the business as well.

142
00:13:02,320 --> 00:13:02,720
Gotcha.

143
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:04,320
So yeah, that's great.

144
00:13:04,560 --> 00:13:07,920
So the pitch was that it's a problem that needed to be solved.

145
00:13:08,080 --> 00:13:09,600
Let's dedicate resources to it.

146
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:18,800
And then once the pitch was kind of approved, you had the workshop to start brainstorming and that's great too that at the end of the workshop you had a specific.

147
00:13:19,920 --> 00:13:23,440
We need to reduce customer service complaints by ten percent around this.

148
00:13:24,720 --> 00:13:35,840
So once you had kind of the early stages of an idea and you had this goal in mind, what, what, how did you start the process of actually building it?

149
00:13:36,800 --> 00:13:40,880
Prototyping wireframing, like how did it actually come into being?

150
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:42,080
Yeah.

151
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:46,720
So for the first kind of test that we wanted to run was with these trial reminders.

152
00:13:46,880 --> 00:13:50,400
So not this like timeline subscription screen people have seen in the article.

153
00:13:50,720 --> 00:14:00,480
It was just having the screen after you subscribe, you have a a modal that would say., do you want to subscribe to these try reminders?

154
00:14:00,640 --> 00:14:04,000
So a couple of days before your trial ends, you'll be reminded and you can cancel.

155
00:14:04,800 --> 00:14:07,280
So that was kind of the idea that we came out of this with.

156
00:14:07,680 --> 00:14:17,360
And so we were using like an external tool and I ended up coding and designing this like modal thing that would come up and ask people to opt in.

157
00:14:18,160 --> 00:14:19,760
And it was funny to with this project as well.

158
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:26,320
Like we thought ten percent people, sorry, ten percent more would cancel during their trial because they see this.

159
00:14:26,960 --> 00:14:34,160
If they subscribe to this notification, we thought that a bunch of people would cancel and we were okay with that.

160
00:14:34,160 --> 00:14:38,640
With that, if we got the ten percent more customer complaints decreasing.

161
00:14:39,120 --> 00:14:39,360
Right.

162
00:14:39,600 --> 00:14:45,040
So, um, I did some kind of like different designs for this, but mainly we just use like a modal that we already had that existed.

163
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:46,000
Gotcha.

164
00:14:46,560 --> 00:14:46,880
Quick test.

165
00:14:47,040 --> 00:14:47,120
Yeah.

166
00:14:47,600 --> 00:14:55,600
And did did you start with, um, user testing or did you actually split test live in, in the app in production?

167
00:14:56,400 --> 00:15:03,520
So for this part, I think, um, I think for this one, we just actually just did AB testing in the app for this.

168
00:15:03,840 --> 00:15:10,400
Maybe I did get some, I probably got some user testing or user research from this, but people were generally, this whole project, were excited about it.

169
00:15:10,880 --> 00:15:14,160
Um, that then we did this is, uh, yeah., an AB test in the app.

170
00:15:15,200 --> 00:15:16,640
What were those early results then?

171
00:15:16,960 --> 00:15:19,200
So you had a separate modal.

172
00:15:19,840 --> 00:15:21,360
So they hit the paywall.

173
00:15:22,080 --> 00:15:24,720
They say start free trial and then they get the modal.

174
00:15:25,040 --> 00:15:25,120
Yeah.

175
00:15:25,600 --> 00:15:31,200
What were the kind of responses and experience and what were the results you started to see with this first experiment?

176
00:15:32,240 --> 00:15:32,320
Yeah.

177
00:15:32,640 --> 00:15:41,920
So with this experiment, experiment, we surprisingly saw four percent less people canceled, uh, which was very, which is very surprising for us.

178
00:15:42,240 --> 00:15:42,320
Yeah.

179
00:15:43,120 --> 00:15:45,840
Because we expected it to be ten percent more people canceling.

180
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:46,640
So the opposite.

181
00:15:47,200 --> 00:15:50,640
Um, and then we kind of had the hypothesis around this that, okay, we're being.

182
00:15:51,200 --> 00:15:54,480
more trustworthy as a company and we're also reminding people to use the app.

183
00:15:55,120 --> 00:16:11,200
But then additionally, this wasn't the goal at all, but we saw that there was an increase in opt-in to these push notifications by 1116 percent, which is totally a vanity number, but six percent to 74 percent opt-in to push notifications.

184
00:16:12,640 --> 00:16:19,200
So that was also amazing and wasn't the goal, but was just still like a wonderful increase that we saw.

185
00:16:20,720 --> 00:16:28,240
So then with those early results seeming really promising, what were the kind of next steps to iterate on that?

186
00:16:29,200 --> 00:16:34,720
Yeah, so once we once we gathered these results and we then we rolled it out to everyone because these results were super great.

187
00:16:34,880 --> 00:16:36,480
So of course we wanted to benefit from this.

188
00:16:37,200 --> 00:16:39,920
And then we started to think about, yeah, what are the next steps from this?

189
00:16:40,160 --> 00:16:44,480
Like how do we benefit more from this because this performed so so so much better than we thought.

190
00:16:45,360 --> 00:16:49,120
And then that's where the idea came of like, let's do this like timeline screen.

191
00:16:49,440 --> 00:16:52,640
And I think this is also from some ideas that we had back in the workshop as well.

192
00:16:53,200 --> 00:17:02,160
So to have a screen, it didn't necessarily a timeline at that point, but just somehow saying on the subscription screen, like, hey, we can send you reminders if you opt into this.

193
00:17:02,480 --> 00:17:05,600
So we will let you know to cancel so you don't have to do it right away.

194
00:17:06,400 --> 00:17:12,160
So that's what kind of started to become this, this timeline prototype, I guess.

195
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:22,960
And then similar, did you kind of follow this similar pattern of building out a prototype and then rolling it out in the app as a AB test?

196
00:17:23,760 --> 00:17:23,840
Yeah.

197
00:17:24,080 --> 00:17:25,920
So for this one, we definitely did user testing.

198
00:17:26,160 --> 00:17:29,600
